WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:04.020
Film Courage: So I'm taking a quote that you have here
"I'm at my best when I'm telling extremely

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.470
personal stories." Justin Warren, screenwriter/director: Hhmmm.
Film Courage: Would you say you're an open book? You're

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:13.259
okay with people knowing things about
you? Justin: Absolutely, yeah. I like to in fact

00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:17.369
I try to challenge myself to be even
more open I don't feel like I'm as open

00:00:17.369 --> 00:00:23.609
as I need to be and what I mean by that
is like I I've always had like sort of

00:00:23.609 --> 00:00:31.500
an outsider perspective growing up and
I've always felt like I have a reservoir

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:38.430
of almost like pain and truth from which
to draw from and I've noticed that like

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:42.480
whenever I'm telling stories that aren't
very personal there's like something

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:50.430
kind of missing about it there's like
there's like it's almost like an athlete

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.190
in the fourth quarter who doesn't have
their legs under them you know what I

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:57.660
mean to shoot a jump shot but I noticed
that when I when I really lean into my

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:02.969
truth and I lean into the things that
mean a lot to me and lean into the

00:01:02.969 --> 00:01:07.500
things that have value and things I can
talk about it at length and just kind of

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.210
keep keep going on and seem to never
really run out of energy those are my

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:18.600
best stories that did I tell and I also
think there's something to be sad about

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:23.930
because a professor of mine when I was
at school

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:30.720
it's a Vincent Robert said this to me he
said why do we tell stories and he asked

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:33.720
that one day and everybody in class was
like raising their hands saying all

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:40.380
these different things and we were all
wrong and he said no no we tell stories

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.869
to heal that's why we tell stories and I
had a moment where I

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:49.530
I almost couldn't breathe because I
realized he was totally right at least

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:56.000
for me I realized that's why I tell
stories because there's something about

00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.270
opening up an old wound trying to heal
it in a way where other people can see

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:08.429
it that absolutely heals them too at
least that's what I've experienced so

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:12.300
like in in in my film my film is all
about these two strangers who are

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:14.939
actually brothers who learned
become brothers that's what the whole

00:02:14.939 --> 00:02:21.030
movies about and it's based on like a
true story of my actual extremely broken

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:26.310
relationship with my older brother and
you know we both grew up like total

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:35.370
opposites like he I was very much a
private-school try to make straight A's

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:43.590
kind of kid and and my other brother
like basically hung out in the streets

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:49.590
and you know had a very vibrant very
successful life in crime and I just

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:56.099
didn't understand it I didn't get it and
it hurt me for so so so many years and I

00:02:56.099 --> 00:03:03.090
knew that like oh man if I tell a story
about that and I can like I can make fun

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:09.629
of myself make fun of all the things
that that are I'll basically make light

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:12.810
of all the things that cause me pain I
know for a fact there's gonna be other

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:16.639
people out there that are experiencing
something similar that can relate to it

00:03:16.639 --> 00:03:21.629
because a lot of times when you're super
specific about the things that that

00:03:21.629 --> 00:03:27.959
caused you pain you can achieve almost
like a universal sense of connection

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:31.500
with other people because other people
can see themselves in very specific

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:37.109
stories and I knew that and that was
something I learned throughout school

00:03:37.109 --> 00:03:43.650
and so like I I've noticed that every
single time I do that every single time

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:48.569
I'm honest and I'm open
I usually connect with way more people

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:52.739
than if I tried to be someone else or
tried to tell someone else's story that

00:03:52.739 --> 00:03:57.989
didn't fit me I didn't I didn't have
like a I always have to find like an

00:03:57.989 --> 00:04:01.739
emotional end that moves me deeply to my
core

00:04:01.739 --> 00:04:06.229
something I can speak about at length
and when that happens whenever I know

00:04:06.229 --> 00:04:12.060
sort of thematically why I'm telling the
story then I can I feel like I could

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:20.010
make a 10 hour movie about it you know
but yeah so I always try to tell stories

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.820
are extremely personal and try to be as
open as possible about it I'm trying to

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:28.340
be even more open about
the just the process of what it takes to

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:34.820
put a movie together and the pain that
goes with that - yeah see people respect

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:40.070
that honesty cuz a lot of us don't show
different things we just want to put oh

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:43.280
no everything's great I got this kind of
you know that's become this new term and

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.720
I feel like we've become a society of
like I got this I'm good and I'm not you

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.040
know yeah and and the reality is like
human beings we are created for

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:57.590
community like we're not really created
I know our society is very individual

00:04:57.590 --> 00:05:00.800
you know we celebrate the individual
like the the accomplishments of the

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.030
individual like I did this I'm awesome
you know and you know there's there's

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:08.480
something to that about like living up
to your potential ISM

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.280
as a person as a as a creator or
whatever it is that you're doing but I

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.380
think when we deny the fact that we need
to connect with other people we do

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:22.760
ourselves and everyone around us a
disservice because something magical

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:29.210
happens when when people open up to each
other and you sort of take a risk and

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:33.860
you wanted to trust another person
something kind of beautiful happens in

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:41.330
that and I think in today's day and age
like it's very easy to like open up

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.840
Instagram and look around it'd be like
man everybody's crushing it but me

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:49.910
and in the reality like we're all super
broken people like all of us are broken

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:54.080
there's not one person on this planet
that's not broken and when you can sort

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.460
of embrace that you embrace a part of
your humanity and I feel like if you

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:04.010
deny that suddenly you're you're trying
you're running away from the from

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:07.460
basically your own fingerprints the
thing that the things that make you

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:13.630
uniquely you and I think the more you
embrace your faults your weaknesses and

00:06:13.630 --> 00:06:17.870
and even your strengths because
sometimes there can be shame involved in

00:06:17.870 --> 00:06:23.510
embracing your own strengths just
embracing who you are the the good and

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:33.890
the bad only makes you a more valuable
contributing member of society how does

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:38.870
it feel to come here
and and want to tell stories you came

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:42.330
from
where it was very embracing and a warm

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:49.590
family that welcomes you but do you feel
a sense of of like freedom well now I

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:53.880
can tell other new stories or is it
scarier or how is that because it seems

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.350
like maybe people really knew you back
